0 @I001@ INDI
1 NAME Petrus /van Duijnhoven/
2 GIVN Petrus
2 SURN van Duijnhoven
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 AUG 1799
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
3 NOTE Registered in Uden civil records
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 MAR 1882
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 OCCU Bouwman (yeoman farmer)
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE GENERATION 1 PATRIARCH (1799-1882). Born 27 Aug 1799 in Uden during the Batavian Republic — a French-controlled puppet state created after the 1795 Patriot revolution overthrew the stadtholder. His father Petrus Johannes van Duijnhoven (1748-1799) died just months before or after his birth, leaving his mother Elisabeth Henricus van Boxtel (1767-1856) a young widow. Elisabeth would outlive her husband by 57 years, dying in 1856.
2 CONT 
2 CONT OCCUPATION: Petrus was a "bouwman" (yeoman farmer/agricultural cultivator) — a term denoting an independent small farmer who owned or leased land and worked it with family labor, as distinguished from landless day laborers (dagloners). In 19th-century Brabant, bouwmen formed the backbone of the agrarian economy, typically cultivating 5-15 hectares of mixed farmland.
2 CONT 
2 CONT THE BRABANT LANDSCAPE: Uden lies in the Meierij van 's-Hertogenbosch, a region of sandy, nutrient-poor heathland (Kempense zandgronden). Traditional crops were rye (rogge), buckwheat, and later potatoes — crops suited to acidic sandy soils. Farmers practiced the "plaggenlandbouw" system: cutting heather sods (plaggen) from the heath, mixing them with animal manure in stables, and spreading this compost on fields to slowly build up fertile "essen" (raised agricultural fields). This labor-intensive system had been used for centuries.
2 CONT 
2 CONT HISTORICAL EVENTS WITNESSED:
2 CONT - Kingdom of the Netherlands established 1813 after Napoleon's defeat; Brabant finally became a full province (previously "Generality Land" under Protestant rule since 1648)
2 CONT - Belgian Revolution 1830 — Brabant remained with the Netherlands while much of the Catholic south seceded
2 CONT - POTATO FAMINE 1845-1847: The Dutch potato crisis hit Brabant hard. Phytophthora infestans destroyed 71% of the 1845 harvest and 56% of 1846. The Netherlands also lost 47% of its rye crop. Population growth in the Netherlands dropped to near-zero in 1847-1848 (−0.2% in 1847). With 0.7 kg/capita daily potato consumption, the Dutch were highly dependent on this crop. Petrus, age 46-48 during the crisis, had to feed a large family through this catastrophe.
2 CONT - CONSTITUTIONAL REFORM 1848: Liberal constitution under Thorbecke granted religious freedom
2 CONT - RESTORATION OF CATHOLIC HIERARCHY 4 March 1853: Pope Pius IX's bull "Ex qua die arcano" re-established bishops in the Netherlands after 262 years. For Brabant Catholics like Petrus, this was a profound moment — their Church emerged from centuries of clandestine worship in "schuilkerken" (hidden churches). The Diocese of 's-Hertogenbosch was created, encompassing Uden. The Protestant "Aprilbeweging" protest against this restoration saw 200,000 signatures — but Brabant celebrated.
2 CONT 
2 CONT FAMILY: Married Johanna van der Heijden on 24 Feb 1821 in Uden at age 21. She was 22. Together they had 12 children over ~20 years, but only 6 survived to adulthood — a 50% childhood mortality rate typical of 19th-century Brabant. Surviving children: Martinus (1829, our direct ancestor), Johanna (1822), Anna Maria (1823), Petronella (1825), Henrica (1833), and Hendricus (1837). Johanna predeceased him circa 1875 (exact date unknown); he survived her by approximately 7 years.
2 CONT 
2 CONT DEATH: Petrus died 13 Mar 1882 in Uden at the exceptional age of 82 years, 6 months. Average life expectancy at birth in 1880s Netherlands was ~40 years; reaching 82 was remarkable. He outlived his wife, saw his son Martinus marry and have children (including grandson Marianus, born 1872), and witnessed the transformation of Brabant from marginal heathland to productive farmland through cooperative reclamation efforts.
