0 @I002@ INDI
1 NAME Johanna /van der Heijden/
2 GIVN Johanna
2 SURN van der Heijden
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 OCT 1798
2 PLAC Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1875
2 PLAC Uden, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
2 NOTE Death date approximate. A WieWasWie search found a Johanna van der Heijden death record in Uden 26 Apr 1872 (akte 56), but this was verified to be a DIFFERENT person: the record shows parents Antoon van der Heijden x Anna van der Rijt and spouse Johannes van der Pol (d.1856), whereas our Johanna's parents were Joannes Henrici van der Heijden x Anna Maria Pittens and her spouse was Petrus van Duijnhoven. See research/JOHANNA_DEATH_VERIFICATION.md.
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE GENERATION 1 MATRIARCH (1798-~1875). Born 13 Oct 1798, one year before her future husband Petrus. Daughter of Joannes Henrici van der Heijden (b.~1712 Veghel area) and Anna Maria Martinus Pittens. The van der Heijden family was well-established in the Uden/Veghel region of the Meierij.
2 CONT 
2 CONT ANCESTRY: Her Van der Heijden line traces back through Henricus Henrici van der Heijden (1667-1722, married Theodora Petri van der Meulen 1 Nov 1699 in Schijndel) to Jan Gerard Jan Lamberts van der Heijden (~1590-~1647) and Elisabeth Willem Welten. The Pittens line traces through Mathias Pittens (b.1 Jul 1709 Uden) and Martinus Pittens (elder) x Maria Tijssen.
2 CONT 
2 CONT MARRIAGE AND MOTHERHOOD: Married Petrus van Duijnhoven 24 Feb 1821 in Uden at age 22 in the restored Catholic parish. She bore 12 children over approximately two decades of childbearing. Only 6 survived to adulthood — the heartbreak of losing half her children to infant and childhood mortality was common among 19th-century Brabant mothers. Surviving children: Johanna (1822-1896), Anna Maria (1823-1901), Petronella (1825-1902), Martinus (1829-1903, our direct ancestor), Henrica (1833-1910), and Hendricus (1837-1910).
2 CONT 
2 CONT LIFE IN UDEN: As a bouwman's wife, Johanna's life centered on the household and farm. Women in Brabant farming families managed the dairy (butter and cheese production), the vegetable garden (moestuin), the chicken coop, and the raising of children. She would have processed flax for linen, spun wool, and managed food preservation (smoking, salting, drying) to sustain the family through winters. The 1845-1847 potato famine struck when she had young children to feed — a terrifying period.
2 CONT 
2 CONT CATHOLIC FAITH: Johanna witnessed the 1853 restoration of the Catholic hierarchy. After centuries of hidden worship, Catholics in Uden could openly celebrate their faith. The Diocese of 's-Hertogenbosch was established, and Uden's Catholics were no longer under a distant apostolic vicariate but a proper bishop.
2 CONT 
2 CONT DEATH: Died circa 1875 in Uden at approximately age 77 (exact date unknown — see DEAT NOTE). Her husband Petrus survived her by approximately 7 years, dying 13 Mar 1882.
