1 NOTE Wife of Johan van Duijnhoven and matriarch of the American branch. Born 30 Sep 1916 in Bakel en Milheeze, a small municipality in the Peelland region of Noord-Brabant, Netherlands — an area of sandy heathlands and peat bogs that Dutch farmers had been reclaiming for centuries. Anna Maria married Johan on 5 Oct 1950 in Bakel en Milheeze — just six weeks before their transatlantic voyage, a common pattern among Dutch emigrants who formalized marriages before leaving their homeland forever. She departed with Johan on the RMS Queen Elizabeth in November 1950, crossing the Atlantic in approximately five days to begin a new life in Minnesota. Like thousands of Dutch Catholic emigrants of her generation, Anna Maria left behind everything familiar — language, family, homeland — to build a future in America's heartland. The transition was profound: from the ancient Catholic villages of Brabant where her family had lived for generations, to the windswept prairies of Sibley County, Minnesota. She adapted to farm life in Green Isle, raising her children in the close-knit community centered on St. Brendan's Catholic Church. The Dutch immigrants brought their strong work ethic and deep Catholic faith, finding common ground with the Irish-American families who had settled Green Isle decades earlier. Naturalized US citizen 5 Jun 1956 in Mankato, Blue Earth County MN (Certificate #7468862) — the same day as her husband Johan. Mother of 6 children: Morris, Wilhelmus, Anna Maria, Peter John, Carolyn Kay, and Mary C. (who died young in 1963). Buried at St. Brendan's Cemetery, Green Isle (Plot 258A-b), beside her husband Johan.
