0 @I085@ INDI
1 NAME Johannes /van Duynhoven/
2 GIVN Johannes
2 SURN van Duynhoven
2 NICK Jan Dirkse
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1539
2 PLAC Aarle-Rixtel, Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1590
2 PLAC Noord-Brabant, Netherlands
1 RELI Roman Catholic
1 NOTE Generation -8. EARLIEST CONFIRMED ANCESTOR (stamvader). Born 1539 in Aarle-Rixtel near Helmond during the reign of Holy Roman Emperor Charles V Habsburg, who ruled the Low Countries from Brussels as part of his vast empire spanning Spain, the Netherlands, Austria, and the Americas. HISTORICAL CONTEXT: Johannes's birth year 1539 places him in the final years of Charles V's direct rule over the Seventeen Provinces (Charles would abdicate in 1556, transferring the Netherlands to his son Philip II of Spain). In 1549, Charles unified the Low Countries succession via the Pragmatic Sanction, making all 17 provinces a single inheritance. Johannes lived through the outbreak of the Eighty Years War (Dutch Revolt) in 1568 when William the Silent led rebellion against Spanish taxation and religious persecution. His death in 1590 came during the height of the conflict - the very year the famous Siege of Breda occurred. Brabant remained staunchly Catholic and under Spanish control throughout this period. Johannes's son Goossen became a Catholic priest, demonstrating the family's unwavering faith during this era of religious turmoil. The 'Jan Dirkse' nickname (= 'Jan, son of Dirk') reflects the patronymic naming system in use before Napoleon mandated fixed surnames in 1811. 487 years of documented lineage. Source: Van Elk genealogy tree (genealogieonline.nl).
1 FAMS @F022@

